{"explanation":"Clamp value into [lower, upper] using comparisons. If value < lower return lower; if value > upper return upper; otherwise return value. Inputs are finite with lower<=upper, so the result is finite. Negative zero is normalized to 0 so the JSON-serializable result is stable (JSON.stringify(-0) is '0' anyway, but this avoids surprises). No inputs are mutated.","code":"function solve(value, lower, upper) {
  var result = value < lower ? lower : (value > upper ? upper : value);
  return result === 0 ? 0 : result;
}"}

{"code":"function solve(value, lower, upper) {
  const r = Math.min(Math.max(value, lower), upper);
  return r === 0 ? 0 : r;
}"}

{"explanation":"Iterate over indices and accumulate the sum of a[i]*b[i]. Inputs are integers in -100..100 with length <= 100, so every product and the running sum are small exact integers within Number's safe range. Empty arrays yield 0. The sum starts at 0 and adds products; to avoid returning -0, any -0 result is normalized by adding 0 (0 + -0 = 0, and the accumulator starts at +0 so it never becomes -0). Inputs are not mutated.","code":"function solve(a, b) {
  var n = Math.min(a.length, b.length);
  var sum = 0;
  for (var i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    sum += a[i] * b[i];
  }
  return sum + 0;
}"}

{"explanation":"Iterate the Collatz process from n, halving even values and applying 3*n+1 to odd values, counting each transition until the value reaches 1. For n in 1..100 the values stay well within safe integer range, and n=1 returns 0 because no transitions are needed.","code":"function solve(n) {
  var count = 0;
  var x = n;
  while (x !== 1) {
    if (x % 2 === 0) {
      x = x / 2;
    } else {
      x = 3 * x + 1;
    }
    count++;
  }
  return count;
}"}

