{"explanation":"Coerce n to an unsigned 32-bit integer with n >>> 0, then use the standard SWAR popcount with Math.imul to avoid precision loss. Each step keeps values in 32-bit range and the final >>> 24 yields a Number from 0 to 32.","code":"function solve(n) {
  var x = n >>> 0;
  x = x - ((x >>> 1) & 0x55555555);
  x = (x & 0x33333333) + ((x >>> 2) & 0x33333333);
  x = (x + (x >>> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f;
  return Math.imul(x, 0x01010101) >>> 24;
}"}

{"explanation":"Empty pointer returns the whole document. Otherwise split on '/', drop the leading empty token, and unescape each token (~1 to /, then ~0 to ~, in that order). Walk the document: for arrays, the token must be a canonical nonnegative integer (0 or no leading zeros, digits only) and within bounds; for plain objects, use Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call so __proto__ and constructor are treated as ordinary own names. Anything else (primitives, null, missing keys, '-' on arrays) yields null. A found value that is undefined cannot occur in JSON, but is guarded to null. The input is never mutated and the result is returned as-is (a reference to the sub-value), which is JSON-safe. A pointer not starting with '/' is not expected per the domain, and is treated as missing.","code":"function solve(document, pointer) {
  if (pointer === '') return document;
  if (typeof pointer !== 'string' || pointer.charAt(0) !== '/') return null;
  var tokens = pointer.slice(1).split('/');
  var cur = document;
  for (var i = 0; i < tokens.length; i++) {
    var tok = tokens[i].replace(/~1/g, '/').replace(/~0/g, '~');
    if (cur === null || typeof cur !== 'object') return null;
    if (Array.isArray(cur)) {
      if (!/^(0|[1-9][0-9]*)$/.test(tok)) return null;
      var idx = Number(tok);
      if (!(idx < cur.length)) return null;
      if (!Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(cur, tok)) return null;
      cur = cur[idx];
    } else {
      if (!Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(cur, tok)) return null;
      cur = cur[tok];
    }
  }
  return cur === undefined ? null : cur;
}"}

{"explanation":"The hour hand angle is (hour % 12) * 30 + minute * 0.5 degrees; the minute hand angle is minute * 6 degrees. Take the absolute difference, and if it exceeds 180 return 360 minus it, giving the smaller angle. Inputs are not mutated and the result is a finite Number.","code":"function solve(hour, minute) {
  var h = (hour % 12) * 30 + minute * 0.5;
  var m = minute * 6;
  var d = Math.abs(h - m);
  if (d > 180) d = 360 - d;
  return d;
}"}

{"explanation":"Kadane's algorithm: iterate once, tracking the best subarray sum ending at the current element (cur = max(x, cur + x)) and the overall best. Initialized from the first element so all-negative arrays return the largest single element (nonempty requirement). Does not mutate input; returns a plain Number.","code":"function solve(values) {
  let cur = values[0];
  let best = values[0];
  for (let i = 1; i < values.length; i++) {
    const x = values[i];
    cur = Math.max(x, cur + x);
    if (cur > best) best = cur;
  }
  return best;
}"}

{"code":"function solve(n,edges,start,end){
