}"}

{"explanation":"Build row n iteratively using the multiplicative formula C(n,k+1) = C(n,k)*(n-k)/(k+1). For n<=20 the values stay well under 2^53 (max C(20,10)=184756), and each intermediate product is an exact integer, so division is exact. Row 0 yields [1]. No inputs are mutated.","code":"function solve(n) {
  var row = [1];
  for (var k = 0; k < n; k++) {
    row.push(row[k] * (n - k) / (k + 1));
  }
  return row;
}"}

{"explanation":"US coin denominations (25,10,5,1) form a canonical system, so the greedy algorithm is optimal. Take as many quarters as possible, then dimes, then nickels, and the remainder is pennies. Uses only integer arithmetic via Math.floor and %, returns a finite Number, and mutates nothing.","code":"function solve(cents) {
  var n = cents;
  var count = 0;
  var coins = [25, 10, 5, 1];
  for (var i = 0; i < coins.length; i++) {
    var c = coins[i];
    var k = Math.floor(n / c);
    count += k;
    n -= k * c;
  }
  return count;
}"}

{"explanation": "Evaluate the RPN expression with a stack. Each token is either an operator (+, -, *) or a decimal integer string. For operators, pop the right operand then the left operand, apply the operation, and push the result. Numbers are parsed with Number(), which handles optional negative signs. The inputs are never mutated because we only read from tokens. Negative zero is normalized to 0 by adding 0 to the final result, so the return value is JSON-safe and consistent. Intermediate results are guaranteed safe integers, so plain Number arithmetic is exact.", "code": "function solve(tokens) {
  const stack = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < tokens.length; i++) {
    const t = tokens[i];
    if (t === '+' || t === '-' || t === '*') {
      const b = stack.pop();
      const a = stack.pop();
      let r;
