{"explanation":"Patience-sorting approach: maintain an array 'tails' where tails[i] is the smallest possible tail of a strictly increasing subsequence of length i+1. For each value, binary search for the first tail >= value (lower bound) and replace it, or append if none exists. Using lower bound enforces strictness. The input is only read, never mutated. Runs in O(n log n); empty input returns 0.","code":"function solve(values) {
  var tails = [];
  for (var i = 0; i < values.length; i++) {
    var v = values[i];
    var lo = 0;
    var hi = tails.length;
    while (lo < hi) {
      var mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;
      if (tails[mid] < v) {
        lo = mid + 1;
      } else {
        hi = mid;
      }
    }
    tails[lo] = v;
  }
  return tails.length;
}"}

{"explanation":"Standard triple-loop matrix multiplication. Result has a.length rows and b[0].length columns; each cell sums a[i][k]*b[k][j] over k. Inputs are only read, never mutated. Values are small integers (dims <=5), so Number arithmetic is exact in practice. Negative zero is normalized to 0 by adding 0 so JSON serialization stays clean.","code":"function solve(a, b) {
  const n = a.length;
  const m = b.length;
  const p = b[0].length;
  const result = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    const row = [];
    for (let j = 0; j < p; j++) {
      let sum = 0;
      for (let k = 0; k < m; k++) {
        sum += a[i][k] * b[k][j];
      }
      row.push(sum + 0);
    }
    result.push(row);
  }
  return result;
}"}

{"explanation":"Copy the grid row by row so the input is never mutated. Read the original color at (row, col). Run an iterative flood fill (explicit stack, so no recursion-depth issues on large grids) over 4-connected neighbors, using a visited array. Every visited cell, which has the original color, is set to the new color in the copy. Visiting by original color works even when the new color equals the original color, because the visited array prevents revisits. The result is a new array of arrays of integers.","code":"function solve(grid, row, col, color) {
  var out = [];
  for (var i = 0; i < grid.length; i++) {
    out.push(grid[i].slice());
  }
  var rows = grid.length;
  var cols = grid[0].length;
  var target = grid[row][col];
  var visited = [];
  for (var r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
    var line = [];
    for (var c = 0; c < cols; c++) line.push(false);
    visited.push(line);
  }
  var stack = [[row, col]];
  visited[row][col] = true;
  var dr = [1, -1, 0, 0];
  var dc = [0, 0, 1, -1];
  while (stack.length > 0) {
    var cur = stack.pop();
    var cr = cur[0];
    var cc = cur[1];
    out[cr][cc] = color;
    for (var k = 0; k < 4; k++) {
      var nr = cr + dr[k];
      var nc = cc + dc[k];
      if (nr >= 0 && nr < rows && nc >= 0 && nc < cols && !visited[nr][nc] && grid[nr][nc] === target) {
        visited[nr][nc] = true;
        stack.push([nr, nc]);
      }
    }
  }
  return out;
}"}

{"code":"function solve(document, pointer) {
  if (pointer === '') return document;
  let cur = document;
  const parts = pointer.split('/').slice(1);
  for (const raw of parts) {
    const key = raw.replace(/~1/g, '/').replace(/~0/g, '~');
    if (Array.isArray(cur)) {
      if (!/^(0|[1-9]\d*)$/.test(key)) return null;
      const i = Number(key);
      if (i >= cur.length) return null;
      cur = cur[i];
    } else if (cur !== null && typeof cur === 'object') {
      if (!Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(cur, key)) return null;
      cur = cur[key];
    } else {
      return null;
    }
  }
  return cur === undefined ? null : cur;
}"}

{"explanation":"Iterative inorder traversal using an explicit stack (avoids recursion limits, though depth is at most 50). Walk left as far as possible pushing nodes, pop, record value, then move to the right child. Input is only read, never mutated. Null tree returns an empty array. Values are normalized: -0 is kept as a Number, and the result is a plain array of Numbers.","code":"function solve(tree) {
  const result = [];
  const stack = [];
  let node = tree;
  while (node !== null && node !== undefined || stack.length > 0) {
    while (node !== null && node !== undefined) {
      stack.push(node);
      node = node.left;
    }
    node = stack.pop();
    result.push(node.value);
    node = node.right;
  }
  return result;
}"}

