{"explanation":"Use a union-find (disjoint set) over n nodes. Start with n components; for each edge, find the roots of both endpoints and, if they differ, merge them and decrement the component count. Self loops and duplicate edges never merge distinct sets, so they are handled naturally. Inputs are not mutated, since the parent array is a local copy. n = 0 returns 0.","code":"function solve(n, edges) {
  var parent = [];
  for (var i = 0; i < n; i++) parent.push(i);
  function find(x) {
    while (parent[x] !== x) {
      parent[x] = parent[parent[x]];
      x = parent[x];
    }
    return x;
  }
  var count = n;
  for (var j = 0; j < edges.length; j++) {
    var a = find(edges[j][0]);
    var b = find(edges[j][1]);
    if (a !== b) {
      parent[a] = b;
      count--;
    }
  }
  return count;
}"}

{"explanation":"Kadane's algorithm: iterate once, tracking the best sum of a subarray ending at the current element (cur = max(x, cur + x)) and the overall best. Starts from the first element so the subarray is nonempty (handles all-negative input). Inputs are not mutated; with length<=100 and abs<=1000 sums stay small integers, so the result is an exact finite Number.","code":"function solve(values) {
  var cur = values[0];
  var best = values[0];
  for (var i = 1; i < values.length; i++) {
    var x = values[i];
    cur = cur + x > x ? cur + x : x;
    if (cur > best) best = cur;
  }
  return best;
}"}

{"explanation":"Use a union-find (disjoint set) over n nodes. Start with n components; for each edge, find roots of both endpoints and, if different, union them and decrement the count. Self loops and duplicate edges naturally do nothing since roots are already equal. n=0 returns 0. Inputs are not mutated; the parent array is local.","code":"function solve(n, edges) {
  var parent = [];
