{"explanation":"Count occurrences of each value in a using a Map (keys are Numbers, so -0 and 0 are normalized via +0 handling). Then iterate over b; whenever a value still has a remaining count in the map, push it to the result and decrement the count. This yields min(countA, countB) copies of each value. Finally sort numerically ascending with a comparator (a-b), never the default string sort. Inputs are not mutated since only new structures are built; the result is copied into a fresh array. Negative zero is normalized to 0 so JSON output is consistent.","code":"function solve(a, b) {
  const counts = new Map();
  for (let i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
    const v = a[i] === 0 ? 0 : a[i];
    counts.set(v, (counts.get(v) || 0) + 1);
  }
  const result = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < b.length; i++) {
    const v = b[i] === 0 ? 0 : b[i];
    const c = counts.get(v);
    if (c > 0) {
      result.push(v);
      counts.set(v, c - 1);
    }
  }
  result.sort(function (x, y) { return x - y; });
  return result;
}"}

{"explanation":"Standard triple-loop matrix multiplication. Result has a.length rows and b[0].length columns; each cell sums a[i][k]*b[k][j] over k. Inputs are only read, never mutated. Values are small integers (dimensions <=5), so Numbers stay exact. Negative zero is normalized to 0 by adding 0 so the output is JSON-safe and consistent.","code":"function solve(a, b) {
  const n = a.length;
  const m = b.length;
  const p = b[0].length;
  const result = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    const row = [];
    for (let j = 0; j < p; j++) {
      let sum = 0;
      for (let k = 0; k < m; k++) {
        sum += a[i][k] * b[k][j];
      }
      row.push(sum + 0);
    }
    result.push(row);
  }
  return result;
}"}

{"explanation":"The minute hand is at minute*6 degrees. The hour hand is at (hour%12)*30 + minute*0.5 degrees, which includes fractional movement. Take the absolute difference, then return the smaller of that and 360 minus it. All operations are finite arithmetic on the inputs, so the result is a finite Number, and the inputs are not mutated.","code":"function solve(hour, minute) {
  var h = (hour % 12) * 30 + minute * 0.5;
  var m = minute * 6;
  var d = Math.abs(h - m);
  if (d > 180) d = 360 - d;
  return d;
}"}

{"explanation":"Recursive deep equality over JSON values. Primitives are compared with strict equality after a typeof check (so 1 and '1' differ; null is handled separately from objects). Arrays must be arrays on both sides with equal length and element-wise equality in order. Plain objects are compared by own enumerable keys via Object.keys: same key count, every key of a present in b via Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call, and values equal recursively; key order is ignored. No prototype traversal, no mutation, always returns a boolean. Note -0 vs 0: JSON-wise they serialize identically, so === treating them as equal is acceptable; NaN cannot occur in JSON input.","code":"function solve(a, b) {
  function eq(x, y) {
    if (x === y) return true;
    if (typeof x !== typeof y) return false;
    if (x === null || y === null) return false;
    if (typeof x !== 'object') return false;
    var ax = Array.isArray(x);
    var ay = Array.isArray(y);
    if (ax !== ay) return false;
    if (ax) {
      if (x.length !== y.length) return false;
      for (var i = 0; i < x.length; i++) {
        if (!eq(x[i], y[i])) return false;
      }
      return true;
    }
    var kx = Object.keys(x);
    var ky = Object.keys(y);
    if (kx.length !== ky.length) return false;
    for (var j = 0; j < kx.length; j++) {
      var k = kx[j];
      if (!Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(y, k)) return false;
      if (!eq(x[k], y[k])) return false;
    }
    return true;
  }
  return eq(a, b) === true;
}"}

{"explanation":"Each of the three channel integers (0..255) is converted to base 16 with toString(16), left-padded with '0' to two characters, then concatenated after '#'. toString(16) yields lowercase digits, so the result is always a 7-character lowercase hex string. The input array is only read, never mutated.","code":"function solve(rgb) {
  var out = '#';
