{"explanation":"Dijkstra on a dense adjacency-minimum matrix (n<=20). Parallel edges keep the minimum weight. Self-loops are harmless. If start equals end, the distance is 0. Unreached nodes keep Infinity internally and the function returns null for them, so no non-finite value is ever returned. Inputs are only read, never mutated.","code":"function solve(n, edges, start, end) {
  var INF = Infinity;
  var w = [];
  for (var i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    var row = [];
    for (var j = 0; j < n; j++) row.push(INF);
    w.push(row);
  }
  for (var k = 0; k < edges.length; k++) {
    var e = edges[k];
    var a = e[0], b = e[1], c = e[2];
    if (c < w[a][b]) w[a][b] = c;
  }
  var dist = [];
  var done = [];
  for (var i2 = 0; i2 < n; i2++) { dist.push(INF); done.push(false); }
  dist[start] = 0;
  for (var it = 0; it < n; it++) {
    var u = -1;
    for (var v = 0; v < n; v++) {
      if (!done[v] && dist[v] !== INF && (u === -1 || dist[v] < dist[u])) u = v;
    }
    if (u === -1) break;
    done[u] = true;
    if (u === end) break;
    for (var t = 0; t < n; t++) {
      if (w[u][t] !== INF) {
        var nd = dist[u] + w[u][t];
        if (nd < dist[t]) dist[t] = nd;
      }
    }
  }
  return dist[end] === INF ? null : dist[end];
}"}

{"code":"function solve(coefficients, x) {
  let result = 0;
